    <!-- 2026-07-28: re-tested the 2026-07-23 comment above (cube 2.5m out
         of reach) under clean, confound-free, fully isolated conditions
         (pinn_controller_node stopped, verified via `ros2 node list`;
         raw 40 Nm to joint4 alone). RESULT: joint4 still did not move
         (position unchanged to 4+ decimal places from home). This
         CONTRADICTS the 2026-07-23 comment's claim and CONFIRMS
         SESSION.md's 2026-07-24 account instead: the cube is not the
         cause of this specific joint4 immobility. Reverted to the real
         position below. -->
    <body name="grasp_object" pos="0.5 0 0.02">
      <freejoint/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.02 0.02 0.02" density="1000" friction="1 0.005 0.0001" rgba="0.8 0.25 0.1 1"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <actuator>
    <!-- Single motor (direct-torque) actuator per joint, NOT a pair of
         position+motor actuators (that was tried and reverted: mujoco_ros2_control
         binds exactly one MuJoCo actuator per joint, matched via this
         joint="panda_jointN" target attribute, auto-preferring a position-type
         actuator when one exists, and rejects any OTHER declared
         command_interface that doesn't match the chosen actuator's type:
         "Effort command interface... is not supported with position or
         velocity actuator" when both were declared). Instead, both the
         'position' command_interface (panda_arm_controller / MoveIt2's default
         execution) and 'effort' command_interface (panda_effort_controller /
         pinn_controller_node's Stage 3 path) are backed by this SAME motor
         actuator: 'effort' writes torque directly with no conversion; 'position'
         goes through mujoco_ros2_control's own internal PID (see
         config/mujoco_pid.yaml, referenced via the pids_config_file hardware
         param in panda_mujoco.ros2_control.xacro), which converts a position
         target into torque on this actuator, the same pattern
         mujoco_ros2_control_demos' Tutorial 3 (PID control) uses.
         ctrlrange matches network/constants.TORQUE_LIMITS
         ([87,87,87,87,12,12,12] Nm) as a hard safety clamp at the MuJoCo level,
         independent of pinn_controller_node's own clip to the same limits. -->
    <motor name="panda_joint1_torque" joint="panda_joint1" gear="1" ctrlrange="-87 87"/>
    <motor name="panda_joint2_torque" joint="panda_joint2" gear="1" ctrlrange="-87 87"/>
    <motor name="panda_joint3_torque" joint="panda_joint3" gear="1" ctrlrange="-87 87"/>
    <motor name="panda_joint4_torque" joint="panda_joint4" gear="1" ctrlrange="-87 87"/>
    <motor name="panda_joint5_torque" joint="panda_joint5" gear="1" ctrlrange="-12 12"/>
    <motor name="panda_joint6_torque" joint="panda_joint6" gear="1" ctrlrange="-12 12"/>
    <motor name="panda_joint7_torque" joint="panda_joint7" gear="1" ctrlrange="-12 12"/>

    <!-- 2026-07-22: gripper actuator. A native <position> type (not <motor>, unlike the arm)
         since panda_finger_joint1 only ever exports a 'position' command_interface (see
         panda_mujoco.ros2_control.xacro) with no pids_config_file entry — matching
         write()'s is_position_control_enabled branch (raw ctrl passthrough, no PID needed),
         which is exactly a native MuJoCo <position> actuator's own ctrl semantics (ctrl IS
         the position setpoint). forcerange matches the URDF's own
         <limit effort="20".../> on panda_finger_joint1.

         2026-07-22 correction: kp=500 (this comment's own prior value) with no explicit kv
         was confirmed live to be badly underdamped for this 15g finger. The joint's passive
         damping="1" is inherited from the "panda" default class (sized for the arm's much
         heavier links), not for a 15g mass — critical damping at kp=500 would need
         c=2*sqrt(500*0.015)=~5.5, an order of magnitude more than the 1 it actually has.
         Result observed live: commanding either open (0.04) or close (0.0) produced heavy
         oscillation that overshot the joint's own 0-0.04 range by ~14mm in both directions,
         which position_controllers/GripperActionController (confirmed by reading
         gripper_action_controller_impl.hpp from ros2_controllers' jazzy branch: its result
         position comes straight from the position state_interface, i.e. real qpos, not a
         derived value) sampled mid-oscillation and reported as a stalled, out-of-range,
         reached_goal=false result for both directions. Fixed by both lowering kp and adding
         an explicit kv: critical damping at kp=30 needs c=2*sqrt(30*0.015)=~1.34; kv=3 (on
         top of the existing passive damping=1) gives a total of 4, comfortably overdamped
         for a smooth, non-oscillatory first working version. Revisit if grasping needs
         faster closing than this produces. panda_finger_joint2 has no actuator of its own —
         see the <equality> constraint below.

         2026-07-23 correction: kp=30 was tuned purely against free-space open/close
         oscillation (above) and never validated for actually holding an object. Live
         testing of a real pick against grasp_object (a 64g cube) showed the gripper
         registering a position/tolerance "success" (fingers stopped near the object's own
         width) but the object was NOT actually lifted with the arm. Root cause: at kp=30,
         even 5mm of commanded overtravel into a rigid object only generates
         30*0.005=0.15N of squeeze force per finger, well under the ~0.31N per side needed
         to hold a 64g object by friction (mu=1, 2-finger grip, with gravity alone, no
         margin for lift acceleration). Raised to kp=200: still comfortably under the
         critical-damping ceiling for the existing total damping of 4 (passive 1 + kv 3):
         c=4 implies a ceiling of kp <= c^2/(4*m) = 16/(4*0.015) = 266.7, so 200 stays
         overdamped (75% of ceiling), should not reintroduce the original oscillation bug,
         while giving 200*0.005=1.0N per finger at the same 5mm overtravel, ~3.2x the
         minimum needed. See MuJoCoGripperController.grasp() (stage4/gripper_controller.py)
         for the matching change: it now deliberately commands a position past the object's
         expected surface (not exactly at it) so this stiffness actually gets used;
         commanding exactly the object's width produces ~zero position error and therefore
         ~zero force regardless of kp. NOT YET LIVE-VALIDATED (this fix was prepared without
         simulator access); if grip still fails, check whether the cube is being pushed
         instead of held (kp still too low or forcerange=20 clamp too tight) or whether it's
         now oscillating again (kp too high after all, reduce and re-derive the ceiling
         above for whatever new kv is used). -->
    <position name="panda_finger_joint1_position" joint="panda_finger_joint1" kp="200" kv="3" ctrlrange="0 0.04" forcerange="-20 20"/>
  </actuator>

  <equality>
    <!-- Keeps panda_finger_joint2 mechanically synced to panda_finger_joint1 in physics,
         since only joint1 has a ros2_control command_interface (see actuator comment above
         and panda_mujoco.ros2_control.xacro). Solver params as-is from mujoco_menagerie. -->
    <joint joint1="panda_finger_joint1" joint2="panda_finger_joint2" solimp="0.95 0.99 0.001" solref="0.005 1"/>
  </equality>

  <contact>
    <!-- Upstream only excluded link0/link1. The other adjacent pairs share a
         joint with collision meshes that touch/nearly-touch by design, same
         as config/panda.srdf's disable_collisions "Adjacent" list (a
         MoveIt2-planning-only exclude list, not read by MuJoCo, this is
         the physics-level equivalent). Missing these produced a small,
         constant, fast-buzz vibration in the arm at rest: flickering contact
         forces between links that were never meant to register as real
         collisions. -->
    <exclude body1="link0" body2="link1"/>
    <exclude body1="link1" body2="link2"/>
    <exclude body1="link2" body2="link3"/>
    <exclude body1="link3" body2="link4"/>
    <exclude body1="link4" body2="link5"/>
    <exclude body1="link5" body2="link6"/>
    <exclude body1="link6" body2="link7"/>

    <!-- 2026-07-23: the "Adjacent" excludes above were only HALF of
         config/panda.srdf's disable_collisions list. The rest of that list (the
         "Never" pairs the MoveIt Setup Assistant found never collide in any valid
         configuration, plus the hand/finger "Adjacent"/"Default" pairs) was never
         mirrored into MuJoCo. MuJoCo builds its collision geometry from CONVEX
         HULLS of the meshes, which are looser than the finer meshes MoveIt samples
         against, so pairs MoveIt calls "Never" can still interpenetrate here at the
         folded "home" pose. With every collision geom at the default contype=1/
         conaffinity=1 and no exclude, those spurious contacts generate large,
         persistent normal forces that pin exactly the distal joints (4, 6, 7) whose
         motion would drive the newly-added hand/finger collision geometry (added
         this same session in commit 4b204d0, which left this block untouched) into
         the forearm, while joint5 (a roll about the contact line) stays free. This
         completes the mirror of panda.srdf. Arm-link body names are link0..link7
         here (SRDF uses panda_link0..7). Finger-vs-arm pairs are made symmetric
         across BOTH fingers (SRDF only sampled one side per pair); this can never
         harm grasping since grasp_object is a separate, non-excluded body the
         fingers still collide with normally. -->
    <exclude body1="link0" body2="link2"/>
    <exclude body1="link0" body2="link3"/>
    <exclude body1="link0" body2="link4"/>
    <exclude body1="link1" body2="link3"/>
    <exclude body1="link1" body2="link4"/>
    <exclude body1="link2" body2="link4"/>
    <exclude body1="link2" body2="link6"/>
    <exclude body1="link3" body2="link5"/>
    <exclude body1="link3" body2="link6"/>
    <exclude body1="link3" body2="link7"/>
    <exclude body1="link4" body2="link6"/>
    <exclude body1="link4" body2="link7"/>

    <exclude body1="panda_hand" body2="link3"/>
    <exclude body1="panda_hand" body2="link4"/>
    <exclude body1="panda_hand" body2="link6"/>
    <exclude body1="panda_hand" body2="link7"/>

    <exclude body1="panda_leftfinger" body2="link3"/>
    <exclude body1="panda_leftfinger" body2="link4"/>
    <exclude body1="panda_leftfinger" body2="link6"/>
    <exclude body1="panda_leftfinger" body2="link7"/>
    <exclude body1="panda_rightfinger" body2="link3"/>
    <exclude body1="panda_rightfinger" body2="link4"/>
    <exclude body1="panda_rightfinger" body2="link6"/>
    <exclude body1="panda_rightfinger" body2="link7"/>

    <exclude body1="panda_hand" body2="panda_leftfinger"/>
    <exclude body1="panda_hand" body2="panda_rightfinger"/>
    <exclude body1="panda_leftfinger" body2="panda_rightfinger"/>
  </contact>

  <keyframe>
    <!-- qpos: 7 arm joints (unchanged) + panda_finger_joint1, panda_finger_joint2 appended
         last, in body-tree order (MuJoCo's own qpos ordering, unrelated to and different
         from /joint_states' topic ordering, which sorts alphabetically and puts the
         fingers FIRST — confirmed live). 0.04 each = fully open, a safer default starting
         state for a future grasp demo than closed.
         + 7 more values for grasp_object's <freejoint/> (added later, same session, right
         after the fingers in body-tree order): 3 position + 4 quaternion (MuJoCo order
         w x y z), matching that body's own pos="0.5 0 0.02" with identity orientation.
         Found live 2026-07-23: an XML-specified <key qpos="..."/> shorter than the model's
         actual nq gets the missing trailing values padded with ZERO by MuJoCo's compiler,
         not the body's real starting pose, so reset_world with this keyframe was silently
         teleporting the cube to the world origin with a degenerate all-zero quaternion
         (w=0), not just resetting the arm. This is why the cube "disappeared" after a
         reset_world call. Any future body added to this model MUST also get its DOF count
         appended here, or the same bug recurs silently (loads fine at first launch, since
         initial state comes from each body's own pos/quat, not this keyframe; only
         reset_world is affected).
         ctrl: 7 arm values (unchanged, 0 ctrl on a motor actuator is 0 torque — holding
         "home" against gravity is pinn_controller_node's _publish_safe_fallback's job, not
         this keyframe's) + 1 new value for the finger's <position> actuator, matching its
         qpos so it doesn't snap on startup. grasp_object has no actuator (free body), so
         ctrl's length is unaffected by the freejoint addition. -->
    <key name="home" qpos="0 0 0 -1.57079 0 1.57079 -0.7853 0.04 0.04 0.5 0 0.02 1 0 0 0" ctrl="0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0.04"/>
  </keyframe>
</mujoco>
